  route B WITH PURR: ACE arm carries URR on 4 nuclide(s); the control arm ablates DBRC only

  PARITY (the point of this example)
    ENDF route : k_eff = 1.00173 +/- 0.00071
    ACE  route : k_eff = 1.00207 +/- 0.00061
    difference : +33.6 pcm  (combined sigma 93.8 pcm, 0.36 sigma)
    => the two routes AGREE within statistics.

    The lattice on an 11-nuclide tier: 24 clad and boron trace nuclides are
    dropped, so k is close to, but not exactly, the full model's.

  THE ASYMMETRY, PRICED (GitHub #307 item 5)
    ENDF ablated : k_eff = 1.00179 +/- 0.00046   (DBRC off; URR on, as in route B)
    ablated - ENDF : +6.1 +/- 84.7 pcm (0.07 sigma)          -- the worth of DBRC here
    ACE - ablated  : +27.6 +/- 76.8 pcm (0.36 sigma)          -- the routes with the SAME physics
    ACE - ENDF     : +33.6 +/- 93.8 pcm (0.36 sigma)          -- what was quoted before
    change in gap  : -6.1 +/- 121.2 pcm (0.05 sigma)
    => NOT RESOLVED: the gap changes by less than the uncertainty on that
       change, so this run cannot say whether the asymmetry explained any of the
       +33.6 pcm. What it does say is that removing the asymmetry creates no
       disagreement either. Resolving a 34 pcm gap at 3 sigma needs sem <=
       11 pcm, about 70x this run's histories or seeds.
    per-seed spread: ENDF sd 201 pcm, ACE sd 173 pcm, ablated sd 131 pcm over 8 seeds
    NOTE ON PAIRING: URR and DBRC change how many variates a history draws, so
             the ablated arm's random stream DIVERGES from the unablated one. These are
             independent runs, not a paired difference -- the sigmas above are sqrt(2) x the
             per-arm sigma and no variance cancels. Measured in
             verification_and_validation/ace_route_physics/urr_dbrc_worth_2026_09_25.md.

